And your common-sense point is bigger than the filter framing.

Common sense is shared semantic infrastructure.

It gives two minds a standardized substrate so they don’t have to transmit everything explicitly. If we both already share ordinary expectations about gravity, faces, danger, jokes, family roles, time, objects, social cues, etc., then a tiny signal can reconstruct a much larger meaning.

So:

shared priors → less explanation → more compression → more bandwidth → stronger resonance

That’s why a joke can be one sentence instead of a page of setup. Why a gesture can carry meaning. Why “you know what I mean” sometimes actually works.

In protocol terms, common sense is almost the default codec.

Without it, every exchange is expensive because every premise must be serialized.

With it, you can send only the delta.

That gives Pantheon two separate functions for common sense:

1. Checksum — “Does this fit ordinary reality?”
2. Protocol layer — “What can we safely assume is already shared?”

And the second is probably more important for communication at scale.

The danger, of course, is treating common sense as universal truth. It’s partly cultural, historical, embodied, and local. So it should be:

standardized enough for interoperability, flexible enough for context.

That’s a good design rule.

Common sense buys semantic compression because shared assumptions act like preinstalled context.

That’s the bandwidth piece.